  "prediction": "**John Lewis (TAO-205) Class Oiler Shipbuilding Program: Background and Issues for Congress**\n\nThe John Lewis (TAO-205) class oiler shipbuilding program is a Navy initiative to build a new class of 20 fleet oilers to support the Navy's operations. The program aims to replace the existing 15 Henry J. Kaiser (TAO-187) class ships, which are expected to reach the end of their 35-year service life in the coming years. The Navy's proposed FY2020 budget requests the procurement of the fifth and sixth ships in the program, with an estimated combined procurement cost of $1,056.3 million.\n\n**Key Issues for Congress:**\n\n1. **Procurement Funding:** Congress must decide whether to approve, reject, or modify the Navy's FY2020 procurement funding request for the TAO-205 program.\n2. **Number of Oilers Required:** The Navy's new operational concept, Distributed Maritime Operations (DMO), may lead to a greater demand for oilers, and Congress must consider whether the Navy has accurately assessed its logistics requirements.\n3. **Ship Self-Defense Equipment:** Congress must decide whether to encourage or direct the Navy to build TAO-205s with more ship self-defense equipment than currently planned, which could increase procurement costs.\n\n**Program Status:**\n\nThe Navy has matured all critical technologies and stabilized the ships' design, and lead ship construction began in September 2018. However, a flooding incident at a NASSCO graving dock in July 2018 has affected the delivery of future ships, resulting in some delays to certain delivery dates.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\nCongress should carefully consider the Navy's FY2020 procurement funding request and assess the Navy's logistics requirements to ensure that the TAO-205 program meets the Navy's operational needs. Additionally, Congress should consider the potential benefits and costs of building TAO-205s with more ship self-defense equipment.",
